  "image_name": "4caf947ea537bec258def852c84411f1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several kinds of electroactive materials and then highlights a biomedical use of one of them. The top section compares how different materials generate or respond to electrical polarization. Conductive materials are shown as having charge separation across the material. Piezoelectric materials produce a measurable voltage when mechanical force is applied, with opposite forces leading to opposite charge arrangements. Ferroelectric materials similarly show switchable polarization, but here the polarity is reversed by an external electric field. Pyroelectric materials generate voltage in response to heat, while triboelectric materials create charge through contact and pressing between two layered surfaces, again producing an electrical output.\n\nThe lower section applies this idea to the brain. A piezoelectric device placed near the brain is activated by ultrasound, creating an electric field. The enlarged schematic of a blood vessel suggests that this stimulation opens the blood–brain barrier, allowing drug particles to move from the bloodstream into brain tissue. Overall, the figure links basic electroactive material mechanisms—especially piezoelectricity—to a therapeutic strategy for improving drug delivery to the brain.",
